1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Cost is where a promising prototype quietly dies, get the model wrong, and a feature that demos

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
beautifully becomes too expensive to run for real users.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
This lesson covers how to build a cost model from token counts and rates, why output usually

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
dominates the bill, and the levers you pull to bring spend down.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Before we model anything, three pricing facts that shape every decision.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
First, pricing is per token. Your spend is simply tokens times a rate. Every token going in and

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
every token coming out has a price, so cost scales directly with how many tokens you use.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Second, output costs more. On every current model, output tokens are priced several times higher

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
than input tokens for the same count.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
That asymmetry matters more than almost anything else.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Third, cashing changes the price. Cashed reads bill at a fraction of base input, while cash writes

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
bill at a premium. Same tokens, different rate depending on how they're handled.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Per token, output heavy, cash aware. Keep those three in mind.

14
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Let's write the actual formula because it's simpler than people expect.

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The cost of one call is a short sum. You take your input tokens and multiply by the input rate. You

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
take your output tokens and multiply by the output rate.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Then you layer in the cash pieces, reads and writes at their own rates, and any server tool charges if you use them.

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Add all of that up and you've got the cost of a single request. That's the whole thing. There's no

19
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
hidden multiplier and no monthly mystery fee.

20
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Once you can compute one call, you can compute a million of them because it's the same sum repeated.

21
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Master the single call cost first and scale comes for free.

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Now let's stare at the two rates because their gap drives your strategy. The input rate is the price

23
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
for tokens the model reads.

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:08,000
On current models, it's the lower of the two, so a big prompt, even a long document, is relatively cheap per token.

25
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The output rate is the price for tokens the model writes, and it runs several times higher than

26
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,000
input. Sit with that.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
The same thousand tokens cost you a lot more coming out than going in. The practical consequence is huge.

28
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:31,000
A short prompt that triggers a long, rambling answer can cost more than a long prompt with a tight, short answer.

29
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
So when you optimize, look hard at output length first. That's usually where the money goes.

30
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Caching earns a closer look because it doesn't just save tokens, it reprices them.

31
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
When a token is served from cache as a read, it bills at a small fraction of the base input rate, a real discount.

32
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,000
But writing a token into the cache the first time costs a premium, a bit more than base input.

33
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
So here's the modeling trap. If you take your cache tokens and price them at the full input rate,

34
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,000
your estimate is wrong.

35
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
The reads should be way cheaper, and the writes a little more expensive.

36
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
To model caching, honestly, weight each cache field by its own rate, not by the plain input rate.

37
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Get that right, and caching savings actually show up in your numbers.

38
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Let's scale one call up to a real workload in three steps.

39
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Step one, estimate the tokens per request, both input and output, for a typical call.

40
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Step two, multiply that by your expected call volume, how many times you'll run it per day or per month.

41
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Step three, and this is the one people skip, find the dominant driver.

42
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Look at where the tokens and dollars actually concentrate.

43
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Is it a giant system prompt on every call, long outputs, sheer volume on one endpoint?

44
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Whatever the biggest slice is, that's what you optimize first.

45
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Optimizing a tiny cost center feels productive, but barely moves the bill.

46
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Three steps, and the payoff is knowing exactly where to aim.

47
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Once you know your driver, four levers cover most of the fix.

48
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
First, tier selection. Run the lightest model tier that still clears the quality bar, since

49
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
over-provisioning simple work is pure waste.

50
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Second, smaller prompts. Trim your system prompt and history down to the tokens the task truly needs.

51
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Fewer input tokens, lower cost, every call.

52
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Third, prompt caching. Reuse a stable prefix, so repeated tokens bill at the cheap read rate instead of full input.

53
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Fourth, batch processing. Send work that isn't time-sensitive through the batch path for a large

54
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
discount when you can trade immediacy for savings.

55
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Tier, prompt size, caching, batch. You'll reach for different dials, depending on your driver.

56
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Let's make tier selection concrete, because it's the biggest single lever.

57
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Think of the three tiers as a cost ladder. The opus tier is the most capable and the most expensive per token.

58
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
You reach for it when a task genuinely needs top-end reasoning, hard problems where a weaker model would fail.

59
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:22,000
The sonnet tier is the balanced middle, strong quality, at a noticeably lower price than opus, and

60
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
it's sized for high-volume production work.

61
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
The haiku tier is the cheapest and fastest per token, and it's the right call for simple,

62
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
high-throughput jobs like routing, extraction, and classification.

63
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
The discipline is to pick the lightest tier that meets your quality bar, not to default to the most

64
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,000
powerful one out of habit.

65
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:50,000
One habit ties this whole lesson together. Don't wait for the invoice to find out what a feature

66
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
costs. Model it before you ship.

67
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Use the count token's endpoint to size your input precisely, without spending anything on generation.

68
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Pair that with a realistic estimate of output length. And remember, output is the pricey side, so

69
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
estimate it honestly rather than optimistically.

70
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Multiply your per-call cost by your expected volume, and you've got a monthly projection you can put

71
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,000
in front of someone.

72
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:23,000
If that number is scary, you now know to pull your levers before launch, not after the first bill lands.

73
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
A rough number on paper always beats a nasty surprise on the statement.

74
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Here's the bottom line. A cost model is tokens times rates, summed across input, output, and cache.

75
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Each has its own price, and output is the expensive one.

76
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Keep in mind, the fastest win is finding your dominant driver, then pulling the tier, prompt size,

77
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:46,000
caching, or batch against it.

78
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,000
That covers modeling and controlling API cost. Take a breath here, and I'll see you in the next one.

